<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    F-22 hardening — opt RKSK out of both Android 12+ (API 31) backup channels.

    Background:
      Android exposes TWO independent "make a copy of my app data" channels:

        1. Cloud backup (Google One / Drive full-app backups, and any
           third-party Backup Transports that plug into the framework).
        2. Device-to-device (D2D) transfer, used by the setup wizard's
           "Copy from old phone" flow.

      Pre-API 31 both were governed by android:allowBackup on <application>.
      From API 31+ the two channels split:
        - android:allowBackup       still gates the CLOUD channel.
        - android:dataExtractionRules gates BOTH channels — cloud
          via <cloud-backup> and D2D via <device-transfer>.

      RKSK's AndroidManifest sets android:allowBackup="false", which is
      correct for API 24-30 (blocks both). But on API 31+, absent a
      <device-transfer> rule the setup-wizard D2D path will still copy
      ALL app-private storage and app-scoped external storage to the
      target device. That leaks the on-disk state that lives outside
      the Keychain-backed encryption boundary (F-04):

        - react-native-fs downloaded PDFs, offline form drafts, cached
          media captured through image-picker / compressMedia
        - DownloadManager files under external/
        - shared_prefs/*.xml written by any RN module we ship
        - SQLite files under databases/ (RKStorage.db is encrypted, but
          its shape + key IDs are still observable)

      Keystore-wrapped decryption keys are hardware-bound and do NOT
      transfer, so redux-persist ciphertext arrives on the new device
      undecryptable. But everything else — plaintext files that were
      never encrypted at rest — flows verbatim. RKSK's threat model
      cannot allow that: a stolen or lost device restored onto an
      attacker's phone would leak PII outright.

    Design decision — total opt-out, not partial:
      A health-worker data-collection app has no local state that is
      meaningful without a live server session. A fresh device MUST
      re-authenticate via SSO/OTP and rehydrate from the backend.
      Copying old-device local files gains nothing operationally and
      only widens the exfiltration surface. So we exclude ALL domains
      for BOTH channels rather than curating a whitelist.

    <cloud-backup> attribute — disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true"
      is Google's recommended belt-and-braces. If the device (or the
      user's Google account) cannot participate in E2E-encrypted
      backups, no cloud backup runs at all. Redundant given our full
      <exclude>, but cheap insurance if a future edit narrows the
      exclude list without re-adding this flag.

    Domain reference (per developer.android.com/about/versions/12/backup-restore):
      root           - internal storage root (files/, databases/,
                       shared_prefs/) — covers most private data
      external       - getExternalFilesDir(), i.e. app-scoped external
                       storage — NOT covered by "root"
      device_root    - Device Protected Storage (Direct Boot). RKSK
                       doesn't use DPS today, but excluding here
                       future-proofs the file if we ever adopt the
                       DirectBoot-aware APIs.

    Verification: see android/DATA_EXTRACTION.md §Verification for
    the ADB + emulator recipes that let you smoke-test that a D2D
    restore actually skips this app.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>

    <cloud-backup disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true">
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
    </cloud-backup>

    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
    </device-transfer>

</data-extraction-rules>
